[See "New Scoring: kickoff posting", upthread, for
context]

This posting lists some primary issue about scoring,
then a boatload of possible scoring system inputs.
It will be considered a given that the game software
will need modification to support the new scoring,
so as you add to this list, do mention but don't be
frightened by potential software changes needed.



I.           HOW MANY KINDS OF SCORES?

Two immediately obvious possible scenarios exist.

* Agree on a way to combine score input components
  into a single, probably large integer output score

    [preferably 128 bit or larger, so that no
    humanly achievable amount of patience will
    suffice to "roll the score counter" with current
    per-component score values]

  keep one high score table, rank all games on (or
  off the bottom of) that one score table. This is
  more or less the present monolithic scoring
  system, though the scoring details will differ in
  any replacement system.

  Pro:

  * There is a single "top" spot toward which all
    parties can aim to compete, making competitions
    focused rather than diffuse.

  * Server maintenance is no more complex than today.

  * This is the "expected" kind of scoring for most
    competitions: one winner, lots of also-rans.

  Con:

  * Agreeing on and implementing conversion
    mechanisms from individual components to a
    single overall score will be "interesting".

  * Only a single kind of excellence can be
    recognized: high score oriented.

  * The fun tournament "compete for lots of
    differently scored prizes" aspect isn't
    included.

* Agree to disagree, and instead create several
  score tables, each ranking game play quality from
  a different perspective that some part of the
  community find persuasive as a measure of "good
  play".

  Pro:

  * More top spots means more "winners".

  * More ways to excel mean more different playing
    styles can be rewarded, from slow extinctionists
    to fast speed ascensions to risky and complex
    conduct play.

  * It should be simpler to achieve agreement and a
    scoring system design if all score components
    don't have to be made somehow commensurate with
    one another.

  Con:

  * No "overall best" game is identified.

  * Many score tables complicates server
    maintenance, NetHack game software.

  * This would be a distinct break with current
    scoring overall as well as in detail.

Does anyone have more pro and con arguments?

Does anyone see a third option, a fourth?



II. WHAT KINDS OF HIGH SCORE TABLES MAKE SENSE?

There's not really much use shredding out which
score components go into which kind of best score
unless a consensus is reached that there should _be_
more than one kind of best score, but it might be
interesting to explore what kind of best score
tables can exist. There's also an issue that while
many might be proposed, some top few favorites, at
most, could be implemented.

* Points scored for monster kills.

  Definitely does invite abuse of "farming", but
  isolates it to one score table.

* Number of conducts kept.

  Probably we'd add more conducts to recognize, so
  this score has more granularity; we might need to
  scale them to make their values comparable, too,
  or do something even more complex to recognize
  that "foodless atheist" is really hard compared to
  "polyselfless polyitemless".

* Fastest ascensions.

  The "Rast-memorial" score table.

* Most kills up to the extinction limit per monster
  type.

  Farming abuse won't help here, but getting all the
  Nazgul, all the erynes, all the uniques, et cetera
  will be important to differentiate the top scorers.

* Most wealth with which the PC ascends.

  The conversions to zorkmids are already in the
  game for this scoring. Multiply nested bags of
  holding will likely be an important method of
  hauling slobgoptious amounts of trash up to be
  counted in this score, as will be finding every
  gem on ever level.

* Least favorable (over the entire game) maximum
  attributes with which an ascension is accomplished.

  Say, something like the _smallest_ value of the
  following sum, where maximum values for each over
  the entire game are recorded and used here, and
  the multipliers are to bring values into rough
  parity:

  (((STR + CHA + CON + INT + WIS + DEX) * 10 )
  + ((HP + PW - AC) * 10)
  + $)

* Racial conduct ascensions.

  Clarifying what this allows for each race will be
  an issue.

* Compassionate pacificist.

  No kills by PC, fewest kills total.

  This has low granularity, competitive scores will
  range downward from 300 or so, but gives a chance
  for fierce competition to best lower collateral
  kills.

* Total stupid ascension tricks accomplished.

  These might need a weighting system to make them
  have comparable value, and clarification of which
  tricks count and how to keep track of them.

Does anyone have other possible high score tables to
suggest, or modifications to the current ones?



III.    WHAT SHOULD BE INCLUDED FOR SCORING?

Many, many more things/components _could_ be
included than _should_ be included, but let's build
a list up first, then cull it down later.

* Singles monster kills, including uniques.

* Total monster kills.

* Lowest monster kills overall.

* Valuable treasure ascended.

* Junk "treasure" ascended.

* Unique dungeon grid squares occupied.

* Unique dungeon rooms occupied.

* Unique dungeon levels visited.

* Monsters sacrificed.

* Altars converted.

* Surviving never-pet peacefuls left behind.

* Ascending original pet.

* Total pets ascended.

* Pets tamed (by monster level value?)

* Pets ascended by "difficulty"/monster level.

* Tins ascended.

* Complete tinned "sets" ascended.

* Unique object types ascended.

  A sop to packrats.

* Levels left empty of monsters.

* Demons bribed.

* Object types identified.

* Objects identified.

* Monsters killed in "interesting ways".

  Drawbridge flapping, boulder rolling, freezing in
  place in water, zapped from within, kicked into
  lava, earthquaked to death, et cetera, et cetera,
  et cetera.

* Artifacts accumulated.

* Conducts kept.

* Alignment followed.

* Racial behavior implemented.

* Traps survived.

* Traps detected without someone setting them off.

* Traps untrapped.

* Zoos/throne rooms/nests/hives/et cetera cleared.

* Experience levels achieved.

* Unique wielded weapons employed.

* Unique armor types worn.

* Unique monster type conversations conducted.

* Oracularities purchased.

* Items shoplifted.

* Pets tamed.

* Sokoban solution speeds.

* Monsters created, many ways.

* Ascending.

* Ascending without dying.

* Intrinsics gathered at ascension.

* Intrinsics never enjoyed throughout the whole
  game (quasi-conducts).

There have to be lots I've missed even in the
present scoring, and many more that others would
like to see included. Suggestions?



IV. HOW SHOULD SCORING COMPONENTS BE COMBINED?

Deferred for now. The existing game more or less
converts them all to gold-equivalents, then to
points.




V.                     MORE?

Are there other whole aspects of the scoring I've
missed? Suggested new categories for discussion?



